Heyang Zhao
    
Quanquan Gu
</span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution</span></span></span><span id="id5" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Computer Science, University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095, USA; e-mail: <span id="id5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qiwei2000@cs.ucla.edu</span></span></span></span><span id="id6" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Computer Science, University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095, USA; email: <span id="id6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kaixuanji@cs.ucla.edu</span></span></span></span><span id="id7" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Computer Science, University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095, USA; email: <span id="id7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xuheng.li@cs.ucla.edu</span></span></span></span><span id="id8" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Computer Science, University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095, USA; e-mail: <span id="id8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hyzhao@cs.ucla.edu</span></span></span></span><span id="id9" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Computer Science, University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095, USA; e-mail: <span id="id9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qgu@cs.ucla.edu</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM inference often generates a batch of candidates for a prompt and selects one via strategies like majority voting or Best-of-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN). For difficult tasks, this single-shot selection often underperforms. Consequently, evaluations commonly report Pass@<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: the agent may submit up to <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses, and only the best of them is used when computing regret.
Motivated by this, we study inference scaling in the more general Pass@<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference setting, and prove that neither majority voting nor BoN exhibits the desirable scaling with <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and the sampling budget <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining the advantages of majority voting and BoN, we propose a new inference strategy called Best-of-Majority (BoM), with a pivotal step that restricts the candidates to the responses with high frequency in the <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples before selecting the top-<math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rewards.
We prove that when the sampling budget is <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\widetilde{\Omega}(C^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\widetilde{\Omega}(C^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, the regret of BoM is <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>RM</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coverage coefficient, <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>RM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the estimation error of the reward model, and <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the estimation error of reward at the optimal response.
We further establish a matching lower bound, certifying that our algorithm is minimax optimal. Beyond optimality, BoM has a key advantage: unlike majority voting and BoN, its performance does not degrade when increasing <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Experimental results of inference on math problems show BoM outperforming both majority voting and BoN.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling law serves as a powerful tool for guiding the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">training</span> of large language models (LLMs), providing insight into how increased training compute, data, and model size contribute to performance improvements.
Originating in the early days of deep neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hestness et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rosenfeld et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, the concept has since demonstrated remarkable predictive power across a variety of domains, including strategic board games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones, 2021</a>)</cite>, image generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Henighan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles and Xie, 2023</a>)</cite>, video modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brooks et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, language generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, retrieval systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and reward modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. While training-time scaling has proven effective, it is also highly resource-intensive. As a result, increasing attention has been directed toward a complementary paradigm: <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inference</span>, which examines how model performance can be improved after training.
This relationship between additional compute at inference time and performance improvement is known as the inference scaling law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Snell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to training-time scaling, inference scaling allows for increasing computational cost in several distinct ways, including expanding the generation input via chain-of-thought prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, incorporating iterative self-improvement, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and applying search-based algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. It can also be realized through repeated sampling, using strategies such as majority voting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or Best-of-<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In parallel, a growing line of works has sought to establish theoretical guarantees for inference strategies. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> provided convergence bounds and rates for the scaling of majority voting algorithms. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024)</a></cite> showed that BoN can achieve self-improvement via a special mechanism called sharpening. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> analyzed the sample complexity of BoN and proposed a pessimistic inference algorithm with provable benefits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While most existing analyses focus on inference algorithms that output a single response, there are tasks that allow for multiple candidate outputs, where it is considered solved if any one of them is correct. This setting is captured by the Pass@<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Building on this metric, we propose a novel <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pass@<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> inference framework, in which the inference algorithm is allowed to generate <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> responses and return up to <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of them.
Since <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, the performance depends not only on generating a diverse set of candidates but also on the algorithm’s ability to effectively select the <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> outputs that are most likely to be correct.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2024)</a></cite> conducted empirical studies on this inference framework and observed the relationship between the coverage and the performance of the algorithm. However, this work is restricted to the majority voting and BoN inference strategies, and failed to theoretically justify the inference scaling law.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As there have been few works on understanding the scaling of the Pass@<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem, we are motivated to investigate the following fundamental question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Q1: What is the optimal scaling of the Pass@<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To answer this question, we derive a minimax lower bound as a function of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that characterizes the fundamental limits of any Pass@<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference strategy, establishing the theoretical scaling behavior for Pass@<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Going one step further, we also aim to evaluate existing inference strategies for the Pass@<math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem and find a strategy that achieves the optimal scaling. Beyond standard metrics like regret and sample complexity, we further introduce a formal definition of <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scaling-monotonicity</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which captures whether an inference algorithm maintains (or improves) its performance as the number of samples <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases. This leads to our second question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Q2: What inference strategies are scaling-monotonic and optimal in the Pass@<math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference setting?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, our analysis reveals that majority voting and BoN are not scaling-monotonic. Furthermore, these methods face fundamental limitations that make it difficult, if not impossible, to attain the optimal regret scaling with respect to <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this issue, we propose a new inference strategy, Best-of-Majority (BoM), which integrates the core ideas of both majority voting and BoN. We establish a regret upper bound for BoM that matches the minimax lower bound, thereby demonstrating that our algorithm is minimax optimal. Please refer to Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for detailed results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of Pass@<math id="S1.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference strategies. Our algorithm BoM is the first minimax-optimal Pass@<math id="S1.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference strategy. Compared with majority voting and BoN, BoM is scaling-monotonic, indicating that the optimal performance can be achieved with large sampling budget <math id="S1.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, making it preferable when scaling up <math id="S1.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve better performance. Additionally, the term <math id="S1.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})</annotation></semantics></math> in the regret of BoM scales optimally with <math id="S1.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, while majority voting suffers from constant regret. BoN lacks the regret upper bound in the Pass@<math id="S1.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem.</figcaption>
<table id="S1.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S1.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Algorithm</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Worst-case regret</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Scaling-monotonic</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Optimal <math id="S1.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scaling</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Majority voting</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S1.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">No</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">No</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Best-of-<math id="S1.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\min\{1,\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}N/k}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\min\{1,\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}N/k}\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">No</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Unknown</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;">
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;">Best-of-Majority (Ours)</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" alttext="O(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">ϵ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">opt</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">ϵ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">RM</mtext><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6">k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;" mathbackground="#BFFFE6" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;">Yes</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFE6;">Yes</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Lower Bound</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S1.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">-</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">-</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our main contributions as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference scaling laws for Pass@<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We show that the minimax lower bound of the regret is <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})</annotation></semantics></math> for any Pass@<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference strategy, where <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the error of the reward model at the optimal response, <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expected error of the reward model, and <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coverage of the reference LLM.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimal algorithm for Pass@<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We propose a new Pass@<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference strategy called Best-of-Majority (BoM). At the core of BoM is a step similar to majority voting that restricts the candidates to the responses with high frequencies in the generated samples, before selecting responses with top-<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rewards. We prove that the regret of BoM is <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}+\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}C^{*}/k})</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\widetilde{\Theta}(C^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\widetilde{\Theta}(C^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, thus matching the minimax lower bound without increasing the computation overhead. With a formal definition of scaling monotonicity, we show that BoM is scaling monotonic, while majority voting and BoN are not.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiments.</span> We compare our algorithm BoM against majority voting and BoN. Our results empirically demonstrate the superiority of BoM against majority voting and BoN and verify the scaling monotonic properties of three algorithms, which corroborates our theoretical results.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation.</span>
We use <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[M]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the set of integers <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\dots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\dots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ind[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ind[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the indicator function.
We use <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the Kronecker delta, i.e., <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=j</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{ij}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{ij}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
We use <math id="S1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y,y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y,y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the elements in the set of response <math id="S1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y},\widehat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y},\widehat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the generated responses, and <math id="S1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y},\widetilde{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y},\widetilde{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the final outputs.
We use standard asymptotic notations <math id="S1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, and use <math id="S1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Omega}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Omega}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to further hide the logarithmic factors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference-time scaling.</span> Compared to training-time scaling laws, the study of inference-time scaling laws has emerged much more recently. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sardana et al. (2024)</a></cite> extended the Chinchilla scaling law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to incorporate inference costs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> conducted a systematic study of inference scaling laws, analyzing a range of inference strategies including greedy search, majority voting, best-of-<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, weighted voting, and two variants of tree-based search algorithms. Concurrently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Snell et al. (2024)</a></cite> analyzed the inference scaling problem by searching against process-based verifier reward models. In contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2024)</a></cite> explored repeated sampling as a simple scaling method to improve performance. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite> studied the performance of majority voting and a variant that incorporates a filtering mechanism. They observed that as the number of generated samples <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases, performance initially improves but eventually declines. They also proposed a predictive scaling model to characterize the performance trend. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al. (2025)</a></cite> developed simple methods to construct a sample-efficient test-time scaling dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference strategies.</span> One of the most straightforward inference strategies is best-of-<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, which has been widely adopted in the inference of language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakano et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For its theoretical guarantees, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024b)</a></cite> established a connection between the asymptotic behavior of BoN and KL-constrained reinforcement learning methods, characterizing this relationship through information-theoretic quantities.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beirami et al. (2024)</a></cite> provided a tighter upper bound for the KL divergence between the BoN policy and the reference policy. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh (2024)</a></cite> proved guarantees for BoN algorithm from a information theoretic view. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> further provided guarantees
on performance when the estimated reward model and
true reward are mismatched. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aminian et al. (2025)</a></cite> extended the analysis to a smoothed variant of BoN. Another common inference strategy is majority voting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> established convergence bounds and rates characterizing how the performance of majority voting algorithms scales with the number of samples. Other inference strategies include variants of BoN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jinnai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, rejection sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and search-based algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pass@<math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> alignment.</span> To the best of our knowledge, the theoretical Pass@<math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference framework is novel and remains unexplored in the existing literature. However, Pass@<math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> has also been proved useful in the training of large language models. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2025)</a></cite> demonstrated that training language models using a Pass@<math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-based objective can lead to improved overall model performance. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> used Pass@<math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> as the reward to train the language model and observe improvements on its exploration ability. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposed training methods to mitigate entropy collapse, which in turn lead to improved performance on the Pass@<math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Pass@<math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Inference Scaling Problem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of prompts and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of responses. We represent an LLM as a conditional policy <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> that maps each prompt <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to a distribution over <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have access to a reference policy <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which, for instance, can be trained using the supervised finetuning (SFT) method. For each pair <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume the existence of a ground-truth reward model <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, which evaluates the quality of response <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given prompt <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During inference time, we can use the reference policy <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate multiple responses. To evaluate the quality of these responses, we utilize an imperfect reward model <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides approximate assessments of response quality. For a given prompt <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we make the following assumptions regarding the accuracy of the reward model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reward Estimation Error)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The expected squared error between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)}\Big[\big(r^{*}(x,y)-\widehat{r}(x,y)\big)^{2}\Big]\leq\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)}\Big[\big(r^{*}(x,y)-\widehat{r}(x,y)\big)^{2}\Big]\leq\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a unique <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}=\mathop{\mathrm{argmax}}_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}{r^{*}(x,y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmax</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}=\mathop{\mathrm{argmax}}_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}{r^{*}(x,y)}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}(x,y^{*})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}(x,y^{*})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the estimated reward at <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is close to optimal, satisfying</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})|=\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})|=\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Combining Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> with Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we directly know <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)\cdot\epsilon^{2}_{\text{opt}}(x)\leq\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)\cdot\epsilon^{2}_{\text{opt}}(x)\leq\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, an accurate reward model is crucial for the post-training and inference of large language models. A common approach is to align the model with human preference data through supervised learning or reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Casper et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>. Since the training of the reward model extensively studied and is not the focus of this work, we directly assume access to a pre-training reward model that satisfies Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we study a novel setting called the <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pass@<math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> inference scaling problem.
Different from the settings where the model is allowed to generate and submit <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> candidate responses, our goal is to maximize the highest ground-truth reward of the <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Specifically, for a given prompt <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is allowed to generate up to <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate responses and select a subset <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> for submission. Increasing <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> improves the likelihood of obtaining high-quality outputs, but also incurs greater computational cost, a trade-off between accuracy and efficiency. We consider the following regret metric:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi^{*}}\big[r^{*}(x,\cdot)\big]-\mathbb{E}_{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{k}}\Big[\max_{1\leq i\leq k}\{r^{*}(x,y_{i})\}\Big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi^{*}}\big[r^{*}(x,\cdot)\big]-\mathbb{E}_{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{k}}\Big[\max_{1\leq i\leq k}\{r^{*}(x,y_{i})\}\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*}=\pi^{*}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}=\pi^{*}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximizer of <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In tasks with a unique correct answer, such as mathematical problem solving, the ground-truth reward model <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> functions as a binary verifier, returning values in <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the regret (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) naturally aligns with the Pass@<math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, since minimizing (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) is equivalent to maximizing the probability that at least one of the <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> selected responses is correct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared with the sample-and-evaluate framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our framework goes one step further by explicitly characterizing the dependence on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. This dependence constitutes a novel focus of our analysis, as it has not been examined in prior works on inference-time algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Verdun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we introduce the reference policy’s <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-coverage coefficient as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle C^{*}(x):=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|x)}\big[{\pi^{*}(y|x)}/{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C^{*}(x):=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|x)}\big[{\pi^{*}(y|x)}/{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the uniform coverage coefficient is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C^{*}_{\infty}(x):=\sup_{y}\big[{\pi^{*}(y|x)}/{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mi>y</mi></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C^{*}_{\infty}(x):=\sup_{y}\big[{\pi^{*}(y|x)}/{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Since Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> ensures that the optimal policy <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic and uniquely defined as <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(y|x)=\ind(y=y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(y|x)=\ind(y=y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and uniform coverage coefficients coincide. Consequently, we have <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)=C^{*}_{\infty}(x)=1/\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)=C^{*}_{\infty}(x)=1/\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Besides the regret, we are also concerned with the following important property of the algorithm, named as <span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scaling-monotonicity</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We provide the formal definition as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, prompt <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and the coverage coefficient <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed. An algorithm is <span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scaling-monotonic</span> if for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{0}\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{0}\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq N_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq N_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and any instance that satisfies Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)\leq\epsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)\leq\epsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the regret satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)\leq\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)\leq\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, a scaling-monotonic algorithm should achieve arbitrarily small regret if the reward model <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is accurate and sufficiently many samples are observed. Furthermore, scaling monotonicity also guarantees that the performance of the algorithm does not degrade when increasing <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, it is a crucial property in practice because the sampling budget <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> can be easily scaled up in hard instances instead of requiring accurate tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce two commonly used strategies for LLM inference, namely (weighted) majority voting (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and Best-of-<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN, Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). We will show that neither strategy is scaling-monotonic by constructing hard instances where the inference strategies suffer from constant regret even when <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, the Pass@<math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem is less stringent than Pass@<math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, since it only requires success in any of the <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sampled attempts rather than a single one. Consequently, the regret is expected to decrease as <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases, suggesting a negative association between regret and the sampling budget <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>(Weighted) Majority Voting</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> (Weighted) Majority Voting</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Reference policy <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, sampling budget <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, number of candidates <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, (estimated reward model <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight function <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Observe context <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             Independently generate <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> responses <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}=\{\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}=\{\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}|\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}|\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              Calculate frequency of each response <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind[\widehat{y}_{i}=y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind[\widehat{y}_{i}=y]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighted</span> <span id="alg1.l7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               Query reward labels <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{1}),\dots,\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{N}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{1}),\dots,\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{N}))</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               Select <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:w(\widehat{r}(y))\cdot\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:w(\widehat{r}(y))\cdot\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               Select <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Majority voting is a simple ensemble method for LLM inference: Multiple responses to the same prompt are sampled using the reference policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math> to make the responses diverse enough, and the answer occurring most often is selected as the final output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> generated responses for a given query. After calculating the frequency of each response <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{i}=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{i}=y)</annotation></semantics></math>, the final prediction is then chosen as the answer that appears most frequently among these samples, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Majority voting has demonstrated strong empirical performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. With a reliable reward model <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be further enhanced by weighting candidate frequencies with reward scores. Using an increasing weighting function <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, the selection rule becomes:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:w\big(\widehat{r}(y)\big)\cdot\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:w\big(\widehat{r}(y)\big)\cdot\widehat{\pi}(y)\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">While the reward weighting introduces extra computation for reward evaluation, weighted majority voting has been shown to achieve better performance than the unweighted version <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.
Despite its empirical success, we show that (weighted) majority voting is suboptimal in the worst case, even when the exact reward function is available, i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the (weighted) majority voting Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with weight function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)\geq 1+2kw(1)/w(1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)\geq 1+2kw(1)/w(1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exists an instance <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the coverage coefficient is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}=r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}=r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)=\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)=\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 9C^{*}(x)\log(2k+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 9C^{*}(x)\log(2k+2)</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm suffers from a constant regret:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\Omega\big(1\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\Omega\big(1\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Majority voting relies on exploiting the reference model’s distribution. Consequently, the hard case can be constructed by designing multiple distinct “bad” answers, each receiving higher probability under <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> demonstrates that increasing the sampling budget <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> or the number of submitted responses <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> does not guarantee consistent improvement for (weighted) majority voting. In fact, when <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, (weighted) majority voting incurs constant regret even if the reward model is accurate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Best-of-N</h3>

<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Best-of-<math id="alg2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN)</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Estimated reward model <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, reference policy <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, sampling budget <math id="alg2.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, number of candidates <math id="alg2.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Observe context <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             Independently generate <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> responses <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}=\{\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}=\{\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg2.l2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             Query reward labels <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{r}(x,y_{1}),\ldots,\widehat{r}(x,y_{N}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{r}(x,y_{1}),\ldots,\widehat{r}(x,y_{N}))</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}|\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}|\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Select <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{r}(x,y)\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{r}(x,y)\big\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Best-of-<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is another effective LLM inference strategy. Instead of aggregating answers by frequency, the model generates multiple candidate responses for the same query and then selects the single best response according to a reward model <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, given <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampled responses <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Best-of-<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> strategy selects the outputs that maximize the reward signal <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{r}(y)\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{r}(y)\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For the BoN algorithm, we have the following theorem on the lower bound of the regret.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For BoN (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), assume that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)\geq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)\geq 2k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exists an instance <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the coverage coefficient is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{r},r^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{r},r^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> suffer from a constant regret, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\Omega\big(1\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\Omega\big(1\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, the regret satisfies</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\Omega\Big(\min\Big\{1,\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}\Big\}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}(x)=\Omega\Big(\min\Big\{1,\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}\Big\}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">BoN leverages the reward model’s signal, but this makes it vulnerable to reward overoptimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stroebl et al., 2024</a>)</cite> when the reward model is inaccurate. Thus, we construct the hard case by introducing multiple distinct “bad” answers that are assigned higher estimated rewards. With a carefully chosen, problem-dependent sampling budget <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\widetilde{\Theta}(C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\widetilde{\Theta}(C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, the lower bound will become <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Omega}(\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Omega}(\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k})</annotation></semantics></math>, which aligns with the general lower bound for inference algorithms (as will be discussed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). However, this lower bound implies that BoN is not scaling-monotonic, as for fixed <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the regret converges to a non-zero constant when <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> becomes sufficiently large. Thus, increasing <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for BoN not only causes higher computational overhead, but can also degrade performance when the reward model is inaccurate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem 3.4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> shows that the regret of BoN can be upper bounded by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}\big(\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}\big(\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\widetilde{\Theta}\big(C^{*}(x)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\widetilde{\Theta}\big(C^{*}(x)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared with the lower bound in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, the regret bound for BoN still exhibits a gap of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{k}</annotation></semantics></math> under the Pass@<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> setting. However, the proof techniques for BoN in Pass@<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> inference problems cannot be directly extended to the Pass@<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> setting. Specifically, their analysis introduces an auxiliary distribution induced by rejection sampling, which becomes difficult to generalize when the algorithm is allowed to select <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distinct responses as in our framework. More importantly, their proof relies on bounding the expected squared error of the reward model under the optimal policy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi^{*}}[|r^{*}(x,y)-\widehat{r}(x,y)|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi^{*}}[|r^{*}(x,y)-\widehat{r}(x,y)|]</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be upper bounded by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. While this quantity does not affect the regret bound in their original setting, it becomes the dominant term in our case, which prevents the derivation of the optimal <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{k}</annotation></semantics></math> regret scaling. For these reasons, we conjecture that it may be inherently impossible to obtain a regret upper bound for BoN with the optimal <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{k}</annotation></semantics></math> scaling under the Pass@<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> setting. We leave this to future work.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference</h2>

<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Best-of-Majority (BoM)</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
            
            
           Estimated reward model <math id="alg3.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, reference policy <math id="alg3.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, frequency threshold <math id="alg3.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, sampling budget <math id="alg3.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, number of candidates <math id="alg3.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
           Observe context <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
           Independently generate <math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> responses <math id="alg3.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}=\{\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}=\{\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg3.l2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
           Calculate frequency of each response <math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="alg3.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{i}=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{i}=y)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
           Eliminate responses with frequency less than <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="alg3.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}=\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq\alpha\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}=\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq\alpha\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
           Query reward labels <math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{1}),\dots,\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{N}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{1}),\dots,\widehat{r}(x,\widehat{y}_{N}))</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg3.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}|\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}|\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg3.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg3.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg3.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg3.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg3.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
            Select <math id="alg3.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}:\widehat{r}(y)\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}:\widehat{r}(y)\big\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg3.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg3.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg3.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg3.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we have proved that neither (weighted) majority voting nor BoN is scaling monotonic, and neither demonstrates the desirable scaling with <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the Pass@<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference scaling problem. Moreover, our earlier analysis reveals complementary strengths of these methods: majority voting performs well when the reference policy assigns a higher probability to the ground-truth answer than to incorrect ones, while Best-of-<math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> can be highly effective when the reward model <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is accurate. However, each method also exhibits weaknesses, as they fail to fully exploit the available information from either the policy or the reward model. To address these limitations, we introduce a new algorithm, Best-of-Majority (BoM), which integrates the advantages of both approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our algorithm is built upon the principles of pessimism commonly used in reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Buckman et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. When the reference policy <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns low probability to a response, that response is rarely observed in the training data. Consequently, the reward model receives limited supervision in this region, leading to higher uncertainty and likelihood of error. The pessimism principle advocates making conservative predictions under such uncertainty, which motivates our design choice: we rely on the reward model only when <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns sufficiently high probability to the candidate. Since <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be directly observed, we approximate it using empirical frequencies of generated responses. Specifically, let <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> generated responses for a given query. We first calculate the empirical frequency of each emerging response:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{i}=y)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\pi}(y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{i}=y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Guided by the pessimism principle, we discard responses whose frequency falls below a threshold <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, retaining only the subset</p>
<table id="S5.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}=\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq\alpha\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}=\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}:\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq\alpha\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Then we query the reward model on the surviving candidates and select the top <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses according to their predicted rewards, <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}:\widehat{r}(y)\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\widetilde{y}_{k}=\text{Top-k}\big\{y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}:\widehat{r}(y)\big\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following theorem demonstrates the upper bound of BoM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the threshold is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=3/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=3/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, and the sampling budget is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 16C^{*}(x)\log\big(kC^{*}(x)/\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 16C^{*}(x)\log\big(kC^{*}(x)/\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the regret of BoM (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+O\Big(\sqrt{{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}/{k}}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+O\Big(\sqrt{{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}/{k}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}(x)\ll\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}^{2}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>RM</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}(x)\ll\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}^{2}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the second term dominates, and consequently the overall regret scales as <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with the intuition that increasing <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> enlarges the candidate set and thereby makes the problem easier. Moreover, for fixed <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the regret bound converges to <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}(x)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>RM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{RM}}(x)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">BoM (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is scaling-monotonic.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Complexity.</span> According to Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the BoM algorithm requires approximately <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Omega}(C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Omega}(C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> samples to achieve low regret. In comparison, Theorem 3.4 in Huang et al. (2025) shows that when <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the Best-of-<math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN) algorithm also requires <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Theta}(C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Theta}(C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> samples. This means for Pass@<math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference, BoM achieves a better regret bound with a <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{k}</annotation></semantics></math> improvement without incurring additional generation cost. Moreover, BoM only queries the reward model for a filtered subset of candidates (see Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Line <a href="#alg3.l5" title="In Algorithm 3 ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), which can reduce the number of reward evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketch of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The crucial step of BoM involves the construction of <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the set of all responses <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following two properties of <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> makes it preferable as the set of candidates: Firstly, if <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have an upper bound of the minimum estimation error <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{i\in[k]}\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{i\in[k]}\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}=|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}=|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{i\in[k]}\Delta_{i}\leq\sqrt{\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{k}\Delta_{i}^{2}/k}\leq\sqrt{\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{k}\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)\Delta_{i}^{2}/(\alpha k)}\leq\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/(\alpha k)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{i\in[k]}\Delta_{i}\leq\sqrt{\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{k}\Delta_{i}^{2}/k}\leq\sqrt{\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{k}\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)\Delta_{i}^{2}/(\alpha k)}\leq\sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/(\alpha k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where we used the property <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in the second inequality. Secondly, since <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)\geq 1/C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)\geq 1/C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, if <math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}(x)=\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}(x)=\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm either outputs <math id="S5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> among the <math id="S5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> submitted responses, incurring zero regret, or outputs <math id="S5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses with <math id="S5.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\geq\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\geq\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the regret can be decomposed as</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\underbrace{|r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})|}_{\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)}+\underbrace{[\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})]}_{\leq 0}+\underbrace{|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|}_{\Delta_{i}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\underbrace{|r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})|}_{\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)}+\underbrace{[\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})]}_{\leq 0}+\underbrace{|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|}_{\Delta_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">We take the minimum, plug in (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In Proof Sketch of Theorem . ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), and obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\min_{i\in[k]}\Delta_{\widetilde{y}_{i}}\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\min_{i\in[k]}\Delta_{\widetilde{y}_{i}}\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">However, without direct access to <math id="S5.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the empirical frequency <math id="S5.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S5.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the construction of <math id="S5.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, making <math id="S5.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> an </span><span id="S5.p5.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">approximation</span><span id="S5.p5.4.3" class="ltx_text"> of <math id="S5.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. To extend the two properties of <math id="S5.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, we require the following event that sandwiches <math id="S5.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}:\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}:\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">Under event <math id="S5.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> can be set as <math id="S5.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In Proof Sketch of Theorem . ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>). The complete expectation formula gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})+\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\neg\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})+\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\neg\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}+\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}+\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.6.1" class="ltx_text">so it remains to characterize the probability of <math id="S5.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The probability of <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊄</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be characterized by first bounding <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(y\not\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(y\not\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> using the Chernoff bound, and then applying the union bound with the crucial observation of <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)|\leq C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)|\leq C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. When characterizing <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\not\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊄</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\not\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, we can similarly use the Chernoff bound in <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}(x)\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}(x)\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the union bound does not hold because the cardinality of the set <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(x)\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(x)\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown. To resolve this issue, we assign elements of <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(x)\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(x)\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> into “bins” <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, each with capacity <math id="S5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(2C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(2C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{j}|x)\leq 1/(2C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{j}|x)\leq 1/(2C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The smallest number of bins is no more than <math id="S5.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> because any two bins with <math id="S5.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{j}|x)\leq 1/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{j}|x)\leq 1/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> can be merged. With this assignment, we can bound <math id="S5.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(G_{j}\cap\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\neq\varnothing)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(G_{j}\cap\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\neq\varnothing)</annotation></semantics></math> with the Chernoff bound, and then use the union bound with the bins, which resolves the problem because the number of bins is bounded.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>General Lower Bound</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish a lower bound that highlights the fundamental factors influencing the Pass@<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem. Specifically, the bound depends on the coverage coefficient <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the reward model estimation error <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of candidates <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. It matches the upper bound in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, which indicates that the algorithm BoM is minimax optimal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a given prompt <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)\geq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)\geq 2k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any algorithm <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Pass@<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem, there exists an instance <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the coverage coefficient is <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r^{*},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r^{*},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Moreover, and regret can be lower bounded by</p>
<table id="S6.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)=\Omega\Big(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)=\Omega\Big(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)/k}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> shows that the term <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unavoidable in the Pass@<math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem and does not diminish as the number of candidates <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases. In contrast, the component associated with the expected squared loss, <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, decreases at a rate of <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This bound matches the upper bound for BoM (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), demonstrating that BoM is minimax optimal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we empirically verify the effectiveness of our proposed BoM algorithm on mathematical reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and Datasets.</span> We use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 (Qwen3-4B, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a></cite>) and Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B-Instruct (Qwen2.5-1.5B, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024a</a></cite>) as the reference policy <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Please see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for results on additional models.</span></span></span>. We adopt AceMath-7B-RM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the reward model <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, a mathematical reward model trained on a large corpus generated by different language models which is selected due to its strong performance and moderate size. We adopt the widely used GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, MATH-500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and AIME24<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/di-zhang-fdu/AIME_1983_2024" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/di-zhang-fdu/AIME_1983_2024</a></span></span></span> dataset as our testing corpus.
We first sample <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories and call the reward model to evaluate each trajectory.
The answers are then extracted from the trajectories and clustered by mathematical equivalence.
For each answer group, we use the average of the rewards of all the corresponding trajectories as the reward of this group.
We also calculate the frequency of each answer group as an estimation of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method and Baselines.</span> Given a specific <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider our method BoM, and two baselines, majority voting and BoN. In BoM, we set a threshold <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and select the <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> answers (up to mathematical equivalence) with highest reward score and frequency greater than <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. In BoN, we directly select the <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> answers (up to mathematical equivalence) with highest rewards. As for majority voting, we directly select <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> answers (up to mathematical equivalence) with highest frequency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Results</h3>

<figure id="S7.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen3-4b_GSM8K.svg" id="S7.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/109;" width="153" height="109"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S7.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">GSM8K</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen3-4b_MATH-500.svg" id="S7.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/106;" width="153" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S7.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">MATH-500</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen3-4b_AIME24.svg" id="S7.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/106;" width="153" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S7.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">AIME24</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Results with different <math id="S7.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> on Qwen3-4B. BoM consistently outperforms the baselines on MATH-500 for all <math id="S7.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and on AIME24, GSM8K when <math id="S7.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is small, and matches the performance of baselines in other settings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen2.5-1.5b_GSM8K.svg" id="S7.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/106;" width="153" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S7.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">GSM8K</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen2.5-1.5b_MATH-500.svg" id="S7.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/106;" width="153" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S7.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">MATH-500</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen2.5-1.5b_AIME24.svg" id="S7.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/106;" width="153" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S7.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">AIME24</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The results of different <math id="S7.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=500</annotation></semantics></math> on Qwen2.5-1.5B.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results with varying <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>

We first plot the results for <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,3,5,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,3,5,10\}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figures <a href="#S7.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> for GSM8K, Figures <a href="#S7.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> for MATH-500, and Figures <a href="#S7.F1.sf3" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F2.sf3" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(c)</span></a> for AIME24.
We sample <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2000</annotation></semantics></math> responses for the GSM8K dataset and the Qwen3-4B model, and set <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=500</annotation></semantics></math> for all other experiment settings.
For the Qwen3-4B model, on MATH-500, the performance of BoM consistently outperforms the baselines. On GSK8K and AIME24, BoM also shows a large improvement over majority voting and outperforms BoN for small <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. These results empirically verify the effectiveness of the BoM algorithm.
For the Qwen2.5-1.5B model, BoM matches the performance of BoN on GSM8k and outperforms BoN on MATH-500 and AIME24. The performance of BoM also surpasses majority voting on GSM8k and MATH-500 with <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. These results show that BoM demonstrates a better overall performance over baselines when <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is small.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen3-4b_pass_at_1.svg" id="S7.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/107;" width="153" height="107"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="S7.F3.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen3-4b_pass_at_3.svg" id="S7.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/107;" width="153" height="107"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="S7.F3.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03199v1/qwen3-4b_pass_at_5.svg" id="S7.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/107;" width="153" height="107"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F3.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="S7.F3.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The results with fixed <math id="S7.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and different <math id="S7.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S7.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the performance of BoN is likely to decrease over all the <math id="S7.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The performance of Majority voting remains at a low level. Among them, BoM has a more consistent performance and outperforms baselines with larger <math id="S7.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results with varying <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We also study the performance of the three methods under different sample sizes. We conduct the experiments on the AIME24 dataset and the Qwen3-4B model. We vary <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> between 100 and 2000, and show the results with <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,3,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,3,5</annotation></semantics></math>. Except for the case of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math> where the threshold of BoM is set to <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.015</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.005</annotation></semantics></math> in all other settings. We compile the results in Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7.2 Results ‣ 7 Experiments ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The performance of majority voting remains consistently low, which aligns with Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, demonstrating that majority voting incurs constant regret and does not benefit from increased sample size. The performance of BoN tends to degrade as <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases. In contrast, when <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 200</annotation></semantics></math>, BoM consistently outperforms both baselines and does not decrease significantly with the increase of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation is consistent with our theoretical results, as BoM is scaling-monotonic.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we demonstrate the scaling laws of the Pass@<math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem by displaying the minimax lower bound of the regret and proposing the algorithm BoM with regret matching the lower bound. We also show that BoM has the advantage of scaling monotonicity compared with majority voting and BoN, which makes BoM preferable when scaling up the generation budget. For future work, we plan to extend the study of inference strategies from the optimization of inference-time performance to the impact of combining the trajectory sampling process during the post-training of LLMs with Pass@<math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will prove Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, which provides the theoretical upper bound of Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
To start with, for any <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote</p>
<table id="A1.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)=\{y\in\mathcal{A}(x):\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq\alpha\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)=\{y\in\mathcal{A}(x):\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq\alpha\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">indicating the set of responses with relatively high probability for <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the definition of the coverage coefficient (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), we have <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as long as <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq 1/C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq 1/C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we will build the relationship between the empirical set <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> as the event such that</p>
<table id="A1.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Our proof consists of two parts:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: </span>We first show that <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> holds with high probability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2:</span> Provided that <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> holds, since <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math>; furthermore, since <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{i}\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{i}\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)\geq 1/(4C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)\geq 1/(4C)</annotation></semantics></math> for every submitted response <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then characterize <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}=|r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}=|r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|</annotation></semantics></math> using the definition of the estimation error <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the regret is zero; if <math id="A1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\not\in\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\not\in\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then using Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we have</p>
<table id="A1.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\underbrace{|r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})|}_{\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)}+\underbrace{[\widehat{r}(x,y^{*}))-\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})]}_{\leq 0}+\underbrace{|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|}_{\Delta_{i}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\underbrace{|r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})|}_{\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)}+\underbrace{[\widehat{r}(x,y^{*}))-\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})]}_{\leq 0}+\underbrace{|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|}_{\Delta_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Combining these parts together, we complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now get into the details of the proof. The following lemma states that the event of <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> will occur with high probability:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> holds with probability at least <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1-5C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-5C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof consists of two parts that characterize the probabilities of <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{1/C(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊄</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{1/C(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\not\subset\mathcal{A}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊄</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\not\subset\mathcal{A}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Part I: Probability of <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊄</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
We first fix any <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Chernoff bound, we have</p>
<table id="A1.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\pi}(y)&lt;3/(4C^{*}(x))\big)\leq\exp\Big(-\frac{N\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}{2}\Big(1-\frac{3}{4C^{*}(x)\pi_{\text{ref}}(a|x)}\Big)^{2}\Big)\leq e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\pi}(y)&lt;3/(4C^{*}(x))\big)\leq\exp\Big(-\frac{N\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)}{2}\Big(1-\frac{3}{4C^{*}(x)\pi_{\text{ref}}(a|x)}\Big)^{2}\Big)\leq e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where the first inequality holds due to the Chernoff bound, and the second inequality holds because <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq 1/C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq 1/C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the union bound to all <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A1.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊄</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\not\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\leq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋁</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\leq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)}\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\pi}(y)&lt;3/(4C^{*}(x))\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)}\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\pi}(y)&lt;3/(4C^{*}(x))\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 1-\big|\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\big|\cdot e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 1-\big|\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\big|\cdot e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 1-C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 1-C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where the first inequality holds due to the union bound, the second inequality holds due to (<a href="#A1.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), and the last inequality holds because <math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)|\leq C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)|\leq C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Part II: Probability of <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\not\subset\mathcal{A}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊄</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\not\subset\mathcal{A}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
We cannot use the same union bound as (<a href="#A1.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) because the cardinality of the set to take union bound <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown. To resolve this issue, we first partition <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> into groups, then apply Chernoff bound to each group, and finally apply the union bound to the groups. This technique resolves the problem because the number of groups is in the order of <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, and the union bound goes through without incurring the cardinality of <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">In detail, suppose that <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)=\{y_{i}\}_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)=\{y_{i}\}_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We start with a single group <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, and add <math id="A1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to one of the groups sequentially. For each response <math id="A1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, if there exists group <math id="A1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i}|x)+\sum_{y\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\leq\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i}|x)+\sum_{y\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\leq\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">then we update <math id="A1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}\cup\{a_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}\cup\{a_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest index that satisfies (<a href="#A1.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>). Otherwise, we create a new group <math id="A1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. From the construction of the groups, we can easily see that the probability of any group <math id="A1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> under the reference model satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{i}|x)=\sum_{a\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(a|x)\leq\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{i}|x)=\sum_{a\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(a|x)\leq\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, the total number of groups <math id="A1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> should be no larger than <math id="A1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> because otherwise, suppose that (<a href="#A1.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) does not holds for <math id="A1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and any existing group <math id="A1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}(j\in[M])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}(j\in[M])</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;4C^{*}(x)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;4C^{*}(x)-1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{y\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)&gt;\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}-\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i}|x)&gt;\frac{1}{4C^{*}(x)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{y\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)&gt;\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}-\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i}|x)&gt;\frac{1}{4C^{*}(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality holds because <math id="A1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(a)&lt;1/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(a)&lt;1/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\bigg[\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i}|x)+\sum_{y\in G_{1}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\bigg]+\sum_{j=2}^{M}\bigg[\sum_{y\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\bigg[\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i}|x)+\sum_{y\in G_{1}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\bigg]+\sum_{j=2}^{M}\bigg[\sum_{y\in G_{j}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}+(M-1)\cdot\frac{1}{4C^{*}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}+(M-1)\cdot\frac{1}{4C^{*}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&gt;\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}+(4C^{*}(x)-1-1)\cdot\frac{1}{4C^{*}(x)}=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&gt;\frac{1}{2C^{*}(x)}+(4C^{*}(x)-1-1)\cdot\frac{1}{4C^{*}(x)}=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality holds because the union of <math id="A1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and all existing groups is a subset of <math id="A1.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the second inequality holds due to (<a href="#A1.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>), and the last inequality holds due to the assumption of <math id="A1.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;4C^{*}(x)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;4C^{*}(x)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We have thus arrived at a contradiction, and we conclude that <math id="A1.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq 4C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq 4C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each group, we apply the Chernoff bound:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in G_{j}}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋁</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in G_{j}}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\pi}(G_{j})\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\pi}(G_{j})\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\exp\Big(-N\frac{(3/(4C^{*}(x))-\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{i}|x))^{2}}{3/(4C^{*}(x))+\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{i}|x)}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\exp\Big(-N\frac{(3/(4C^{*}(x))-\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{i}|x))^{2}}{3/(4C^{*}(x))+\pi_{\text{ref}}(G_{i}|x)}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq e^{-N/(20C^{*}(x))}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq e^{-N/(20C^{*}(x))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality holds because if the frequency of one response in <math id="A1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than <math id="A1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, then the total frequency of group <math id="A1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> should be larger than <math id="A1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>; the second inequality holds due to the Chernoff bound; the last inequality holds due to (<a href="#A1.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>). Applying the union bound to all groups,</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\not\subset\mathcal{A}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊄</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\not\subset\mathcal{A}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋁</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in\mathcal{Y}\backslash\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{j=1}^{M}\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in G_{j}}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋁</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{j=1}^{M}\mathbb{P}\bigg(\bigvee_{y\in G_{j}}\ind[\widehat{\pi}(y)\geq 3/(4C^{*}(x))]\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq Me^{-N/(20C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq Me^{-N/(20C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 4C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 4C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality holds due to the union bound, the second inequality holds due to (<a href="#A1.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>), and the last inequality holds because <math id="A1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq 4C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq 4C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e^{-N/(20C^{*}(x))}\leq e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-N/(20C^{*}(x))}\leq e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining (<a href="#A1.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) and (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>), using the union bound, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\geq 1-5Ce^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\geq 1-5Ce^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, we have completed the proof of Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Using this lemma, we then proceed with the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> holds. If <math id="A1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is included in the submitted responses, then the regret is <math id="A1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We now consider the case where <math id="A1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is not submitted. According to the definition of the coverage coefficient, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)\geq\pi^{*}(y^{*}|x)/C^{*}(x)\geq 1/C^{*}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*}|x)\geq\pi^{*}(y^{*}|x)/C^{*}(x)\geq 1/C^{*}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">so <math id="A1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, since <math id="A1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{1/C^{*}(x)}(x)\subset\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> holds, we have <math id="A1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is not selected as the output, we know that (i) at least <math id="A1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses are submitted because otherwise all responses in <math id="A1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math> would be submitted, and (ii) <math id="A1.p11.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{r}(x,y^{*}*)\leq\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}(x,y^{*}*)\leq\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math>. We thus have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\geq\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})\geq r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\geq\widehat{r}(x,y^{*})\geq r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the second inequality holds due to Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Therefore, the regret conditioned on event <math id="A1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{i\in[k]}\{r^{*}(x,y^{*})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{i\in[k]}\{r^{*}(x,y^{*})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\min_{i\in[k]}\{\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r_{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\min_{i\in[k]}\{\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r_{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r_{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r_{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\pi_{\text{ref}}(\widetilde{y}_{i}|x)|\widehat{r}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})-r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)}{k}\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)|\widehat{r}(x,y)-r^{*}(x,y)|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)}{k}\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)|\widehat{r}(x,y)-r^{*}(x,y)|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.4.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality holds due to (<a href="#A1.E8" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>), the second inequality holds because the minimum is no larger than the average, the third inequality holds because <math id="A1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq 1/(4C^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(y|x)\geq 1/(4C^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{Y}}_{3/(4C^{*}(x))}\subset\mathcal{Y}_{1/(4C^{*}(x))}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the fourth inequality holds because <math id="A1.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of <math id="A1.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the last equality holds due to the definition of the estimation error <math id="A1.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining (<a href="#A1.E9" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>) with the case where <math id="A1.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the regret is <math id="A1.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that under condition <math id="A1.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.5.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we take the complete expectation of the regret:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})+\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\neg\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})+\mathbb{E}\Big[r^{*}(x,y^{*})-\max_{i\in[k]}r^{*}(x,\widetilde{y}_{i})\Big|\neg\mathcal{E}\Big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\bigg(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}\bigg)\cdot\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})+1\cdot\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\bigg(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}\bigg)\cdot\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})+1\cdot\mathbb{P}(\neg\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}+5C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+\sqrt{\frac{4C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}{k}}+5C^{*}(x)e^{-N/(32C^{*}(x))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.6.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality holds due to (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.10</span></a>) and <math id="A1.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Regret}(x)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Regret}(x)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second inequality holds because <math id="A1.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and due to Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>. Finally, when <math id="A1.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 16C^{*}(x)\log\big(kC^{*}(x)/\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 16C^{*}(x)\log\big(kC^{*}(x)/\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+O\Big(\sqrt{{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}/{k}}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)\leq\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+O\Big(\sqrt{{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}/{k}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p11.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.7.1" class="ltx_text">We complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Optimal Algorithm for Pass@k Inference ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proof of Lower Bounds</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will prove the lower bounds used in the main text of this paper. Specifically, we establish the results for majority voting (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), Best-of-<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), and the general case of Pass@<math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference algorithms (Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>). Before proceeding, we first establish an independent lower bound regarding <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. This result is general and can be applied to any subsequent lower bound, introducing an additional <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> term.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Lower Bound of <math id="A2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first study the following hard case where any algorithm for the Pass@<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem suffers from the regret of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining this lower bound with any algorithm-dependent lower bound <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> (obtained from the analysis of a hard instance), we can show that the lower bound of the algorithm is</p>
<table id="A2.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega(\max\{\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x),b\})=\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+b)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega(\max\{\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x),b\})=\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)+b).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)\leq\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)\leq\sqrt{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)\geq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)\geq 2k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists an instance <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the coverage coefficient is <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r^{*},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r^{*},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Reward Estimation Error). ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2. ‣ 3 Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling Problem ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Furthermore, for any prompt <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the regret of any algorithm for the Pass@<math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> inference problem satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)=\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)=\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}}(x)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For simplicity, we omit the prompt <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in our proof. We apply the idea of averaging hammer, and consider a total of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> hard instances such that no algorithm can perform well on all instances. The responses set is <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{0},y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{0},y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> hard instances. The reference policy and the approximate reward model are also shared by all instances:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-M/C^{*},\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{1})=\cdots\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{M})=1/C^{*};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-M/C^{*},\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{1})=\cdots\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{M})=1/C^{*};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{1})=\cdots=\widehat{r}(y_{M})=1-\epsilon_{\text{opt}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{1})=\cdots=\widehat{r}(y_{M})=1-\epsilon_{\text{opt}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The hard instances are different only in the ground-truth reward model and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{j}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi_{j}^{*},r_{j}^{*},\widehat{r},\pi_{\text{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{j}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi_{j}^{*},r_{j}^{*},\widehat{r},\pi_{\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[M]</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{j}^{*}(y_{i})=\delta_{ij},\quad r_{j}^{*}(y_{i})=\begin{cases}0&amp;i=0;\\
1&amp;i=j;\\
1-\epsilon_{\text{opt}}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{j}^{*}(y_{i})=\delta_{ij},\quad r_{j}^{*}(y_{i})=\begin{cases}0&amp;i=0;\\
1&amp;i=j;\\
1-\epsilon_{\text{opt}}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">For all hard cases, the total estimation error is <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}^{2}/C*\leq\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}^{2}/C*\leq\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Among these <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> hard instances, any algorithm that outputs up to <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses will fail to output the optimal response in at least <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M-k</annotation></semantics></math> instances, inducing the regret of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the average regret of these <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> instances is at least</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\frac{M-k}{M}\epsilon_{\text{opt}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\frac{M-k}{M}\epsilon_{\text{opt}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Setting <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Regret}=\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Regret}=\Omega(\epsilon_{\text{opt}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> (Lower Bound of Majority Voting)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">For simplicity, we omit the prompt <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in our proof. Consider the following hard instance. The size of the response set is <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+k</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{y_{0},y^{*},y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{y_{0},y^{*},y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The ground truth reward satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{*}(y_{0})=0;\qquad r^{*}(y^{*})=1;\qquad r^{*}(y_{i})=1/2,\quad\forall 1\leq i\leq k." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{*}(y_{0})=0;\qquad r^{*}(y^{*})=1;\qquad r^{*}(y_{i})=1/2,\quad\forall 1\leq i\leq k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, the optimal policy <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}(y_{0})=0;\qquad\pi^{*}(y^{*})=1;\qquad\pi^{*}(y_{i})=0,\quad." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}(y_{0})=0;\qquad\pi^{*}(y^{*})=1;\qquad\pi^{*}(y_{i})=0,\quad.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">In this instance, we assume that the estimated reward function <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is accurate. Let <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=2w(1)/w(1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=2w(1)/w(1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. We further define the reference policy as:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-(1+\eta k)/C^{*};\qquad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*})=1/C^{*};\qquad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i})=\eta/C^{*},\quad\forall 1\leq i\leq k." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-(1+\eta k)/C^{*};\qquad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*})=1/C^{*};\qquad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i})=\eta/C^{*},\quad\forall 1\leq i\leq k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">The reference polity is well defined as long as <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}\geq 1+2kw(1)/w(1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}\geq 1+2kw(1)/w(1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we consider the sampled responses <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\widehat{y}_{2},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N^{*}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{j}=y^{*});\qquad N_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{j}=y_{i}),\quad\forall i\in[k]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝟙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N^{*}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{j}=y^{*});\qquad N_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\ind(\widehat{y}_{j}=y_{i}),\quad\forall i\in[k].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text">Then the expectations of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[N^{*}]=\frac{N}{C^{*}};\qquad\mathbb{E}[N_{i}]=\frac{\eta N}{C^{*}},\quad\forall 1\leq i\leq k." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[N^{*}]=\frac{N}{C^{*}};\qquad\mathbb{E}[N_{i}]=\frac{\eta N}{C^{*}},\quad\forall 1\leq i\leq k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text">Using the Chernoff bounds, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\Big[\frac{N^{*}}{N}\geq\frac{3}{2C^{*}}\Big]\leq\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big),\quad\mathbb{P}\Big[\frac{N_{i}}{N}\leq\frac{3\eta}{4C^{*}}\Big]\leq\exp\Big(\frac{-N\eta}{4C^{*}}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\Big[\frac{N^{*}}{N}\geq\frac{3}{2C^{*}}\Big]\leq\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big),\quad\mathbb{P}\Big[\frac{N_{i}}{N}\leq\frac{3\eta}{4C^{*}}\Big]\leq\exp\Big(\frac{-N\eta}{4C^{*}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.7.1" class="ltx_text">Denote <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> as the event such that</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx51" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{N^{*}}{N}\leq\frac{3}{2C^{*}};\qquad\frac{N_{i}}{N}\geq\frac{3\eta}{4C^{*}},\quad\forall i\in[k]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{N^{*}}{N}\leq\frac{3}{2C^{*}};\qquad\frac{N_{i}}{N}\geq\frac{3\eta}{4C^{*}},\quad\forall i\in[k].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.8.1" class="ltx_text">Taking the union bound with (<a href="#A2.E1" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ B.2 Proof of Theorem (Lower Bound of Majority Voting) ‣ Appendix B Proof of Lower Bounds ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx52" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\geq 1-\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big)-k\exp\Big(\frac{-N\eta}{4C^{*}}\Big)\geq 1-(k+1)\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\geq 1-\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big)-k\exp\Big(\frac{-N\eta}{4C^{*}}\Big)\geq 1-(k+1)\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.9.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Under event <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx53" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{w(1/2)N_{i}}{w(1)N^{*}}=\frac{N_{i}/N}{N^{*}/N}\cdot\frac{w(1/2)}{w(1)}\geq\frac{3\eta/(4C^{*})}{3/(2C^{*})}\cdot\frac{2}{\eta}=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>η</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{w(1/2)N_{i}}{w(1)N^{*}}=\frac{N_{i}/N}{N^{*}/N}\cdot\frac{w(1/2)}{w(1)}\geq\frac{3\eta/(4C^{*})}{3/(2C^{*})}\cdot\frac{2}{\eta}=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.10.1" class="ltx_text">where the inequality holds due to the definition of the event <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and the definition of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, conditioned on event <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, the (weighted) majority voting (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1 (Weighted) Majority Voting ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) will output <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{1},\ldots,y_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1},\ldots,y_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and suffer from a 1/2 regret. To summarize, the regret satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx54" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}\geq\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\cdot\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Regret}|\mathcal{E}]\geq\frac{1}{2}\bigg(1-(k+1)\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big)\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}\geq\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E})\cdot\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Regret}|\mathcal{E}]\geq\frac{1}{2}\bigg(1-(k+1)\exp\Big(\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big)\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.11.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 9C^{*}(x)\log(2k+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 9C^{*}(x)\log(2k+2)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx55" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1-(k+1)\exp\Big[\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big]\geq 1/2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-(k+1)\exp\Big[\frac{-N}{9C^{*}}\Big]\geq 1/2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.12.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> (Lower Bound of BoN)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we construct two hard instances to accommodate two cases: (i) When <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is small, then it is very likely that <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> does not even appear in <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; (ii) When <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is large, then it is very likely that a number of responses that are suboptimal in <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> but better than <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled. The two hard instances share the same structure but are different in parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Best-of-N ‣ 4 Suboptimality of Existing Inference Strategies ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For simplicity, we omit the prompt <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider two hard instances, one for <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other for <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:</span> <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider a hard instance with <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{y_{0},y^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{y_{0},y^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="A2.EGx56" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}(y_{0})=0,\quad\pi^{*}(y^{*})=1;\qquad r^{*}(y_{0})=0,\quad r^{*}(y^{*})=1;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}(y_{0})=0,\quad\pi^{*}(y^{*})=1;\qquad r^{*}(y_{0})=0,\quad r^{*}(y^{*})=1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-1/C^{*},\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*})=1/C^{*};\qquad\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y^{*})=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-1/C^{*},\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*})=1/C^{*};\qquad\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y^{*})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For this instance, the estimation errors are <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{opt}}=\epsilon_{\text{RM}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{opt}}=\epsilon_{\text{RM}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If no sample in <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the regret is <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability that <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\notin\{\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\notin\{\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-1/C^{*})^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-1/C^{*})^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx57" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq(1-1/C^{*})^{N}\geq(1-1/C^{*})^{C^{*}}\geq 1/4," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq(1-1/C^{*})^{N}\geq(1-1/C^{*})^{C^{*}}\geq 1/4,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where the second inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the BoN algorithm incurs constant regret in this hard instance when <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:</span> <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider the following hard instance: The response set is <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{y^{*},y_{0},y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{y^{*},y_{0},y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a parameter to be determined. The reward models are</p>
<table id="A2.EGx58" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{*}(y^{*})=1,\quad r^{*}(y_{0})=0,\quad r^{*}(y_{i})=1-\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{*}(y^{*})=1,\quad r^{*}(y_{0})=0,\quad r^{*}(y_{i})=1-\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(y^{*})=1-\delta,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{i})=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(y^{*})=1-\delta,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{i})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&lt;\epsilon_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&lt;\epsilon_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sufficiently small positive number to ensure that the reward of <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\dots,y_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\dots,y_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is slightly better than <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is still the optimal response in <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way, <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(y^{*})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(y^{*})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(y_{i})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(y_{i})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1,\dots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1,\dots,M</annotation></semantics></math>. The reference model satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.EGx59" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*})=1/C^{*},\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-1/C^{*}-p,\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i})=p/M." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y^{*})=1/C^{*},\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-1/C^{*}-p,\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{i})=p/M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">For this instance, the coverage is <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the estimation error is less than <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simple analysis.</span> We first consider a simple setting where <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=k</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> covers every response in <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{1},\dots,y_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1},\dots,y_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{1},\dots,y_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1},\dots,y_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> will be the output of BoN, causing the regret of <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}/2\sqrt{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}/2\sqrt{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability of any <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> not being covered is</p>
<table id="A2.EGx60" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-p/k)^{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-p/k)^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Using the union bound, the probability that there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> not being coverer is upper bounded by</p>
<table id="A2.EGx61" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\exists i,y_{i}\notin\{\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}]\leq k(1-p/k)^{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\exists i,y_{i}\notin\{\widehat{y}_{1},\ldots,\widehat{y}_{N}\}]\leq k(1-p/k)^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, the regret of making the wrong decisions in <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\ldots,y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\ldots,y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower bounded by</p>
<table id="A2.EGx62" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1-k(1-p/k)^{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-k(1-p/k)^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">Then the regret satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.EGx63" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\big(1-k(1-p/k)^{N}\big)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\big(1-k(1-p/k)^{N}\big)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.5" class="ltx_p">In this instance, when <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/[k\log(2k)]}/2&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/[k\log(2k)]}/2&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, we select <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(k/N)\cdot\log(2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(k/N)\cdot\log(2k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx64" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1-k(1-p/k)^{N}\geq 1/2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-k(1-p/k)^{N}\geq 1/2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.6" class="ltx_p">and thus the regret can be lower bounded by <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/(k\log k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/(k\log k)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, let <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/4</annotation></semantics></math>. And the regret can be lower bounded by <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx65" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}\geq\Omega\Big(\min\Big\{1,\sqrt{{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}/{(k\log k)}}\Big\}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}\geq\Omega\Big(\min\Big\{1,\sqrt{{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}/{(k\log k)}}\Big\}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This analysis will lead to an additional logarithmic term on <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which is unnecessary. To avoid this term, we consider the following improved analysis.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improved analysis.</span> We consider the instance where <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the event where at least <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses among <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\dots,y_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\dots,y_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are covered by <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}(y_{i})&gt;\widehat{r}(y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}(y_{i})&gt;\widehat{r}(y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,M</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal responses <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is not included in <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which also incurs the regret of <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}/(2\sqrt{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}/(2\sqrt{p})</annotation></semantics></math>. We now consider the probability of this event. Define the following random variables:</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of samples within <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\dots,y_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\dots,y_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A2.EGx66" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle S=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\ind[\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\ind[\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as the occupancy of <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A2.EGx67" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle O_{j}=\bigvee_{i=1}^{N}\ind[\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>𝟙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O_{j}=\bigvee_{i=1}^{N}\ind[\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> as the total occupancy of <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A2.EGx68" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D=\sum_{j=1}^{M}O_{j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D=\sum_{j=1}^{M}O_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to lower bound <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(D\geq k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(D\geq k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the total expectation formula, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx69" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(D\geq k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(D\geq k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{s\geq k}\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)\mathbb{P}(S=s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{s\geq k}\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)\mathbb{P}(S=s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\sum_{s\geq s_{0}}\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)\mathbb{P}(S=s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\sum_{s\geq s_{0}}\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)\mathbb{P}(S=s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})\mathbb{P}(S\geq s_{0})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})\mathbb{P}(S\geq s_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">where the first inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\geq k</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)\geq\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)\geq\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then calculate the two probabilities separately. We first use the Chernoff bound to characterize <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(S\geq s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(S\geq s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. The expectation of <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A2.EGx70" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[S]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{P}(\widehat{y}_{i}\in\{y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\})=Np." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[S]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{P}(\widehat{y}_{i}\in\{y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\})=Np.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.4" class="ltx_p">Then by the Chernoff bound, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx71" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(S\geq s_{0})\geq 1-\exp\Big(-\frac{(Np-s_{0})^{2}}{2Np}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(S\geq s_{0})\geq 1-\exp\Big(-\frac{(Np-s_{0})^{2}}{2Np}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.5" class="ltx_p">We then calculate the conditional probability <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, and we assume without loss of generality that <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{s_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{1},\dots,\widehat{y}_{s_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> fall within <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1},\dots,y_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conditioned on this event <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j})=1/M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j})=1/M</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>. Although we cannot use the vanilla Chernoff bound to bound <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use the Chernoff bound for <span id="A2.SS3.p7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">negatively-correlated</span> random variables to bound the probability. We first calculate the expectation of <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, which is</p>
<table id="A2.EGx72" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[D|S=s_{0}]=M\mathbb{E}[O_{j}]=M(1-\mathbb{P}[\widehat{y}_{i}\neq y_{j},\forall i\in[s_{0}]])=M(1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[D|S=s_{0}]=M\mathbb{E}[O_{j}]=M(1-\mathbb{P}[\widehat{y}_{i}\neq y_{j},\forall i\in[s_{0}]])=M(1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.6" class="ltx_p">We then verify that <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{1},\dots,O_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{1},\dots,O_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are negatively correlated, which is to show that for any subset <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\subset[M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\subset[M]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\prod_{j\in\mathcal{J}}O_{j}]\leq\prod_{j\in\mathcal{J}}\mathbb{E}[O_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\prod_{j\in\mathcal{J}}O_{j}]\leq\prod_{j\in\mathcal{J}}\mathbb{E}[O_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}(O_{j}=1,\forall j\in\mathcal{J})\leq\prod_{j\in\mathcal{J}}\mathbb{P}(O_{j}=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(O_{j}=1,\forall j\in\mathcal{J})\leq\prod_{j\in\mathcal{J}}\mathbb{P}(O_{j}=1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove by induction with respect to the cardinality of <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inequality is trivial When <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{J}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{J}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that the inequality holds for all <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{J}|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{J}|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. It then suffices to show the inequality holds for <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}=[n+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}=[n+1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that</p>
<table id="A2.EGx73" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n+1}=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n+1}=1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)-\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1|O_{n+1}=0)\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)-\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1|O_{n+1}=0)\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=0)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\big[\mathbb{P}(O_{n}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)-\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1|O_{n+1}=0)\big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\big[\mathbb{P}(O_{n}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)-\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1|O_{n+1}=0)\big]\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.7" class="ltx_p">Using the induction hypothesis, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx74" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=1)\leq\prod_{j=1}^{n+1}\mathbb{P}(O_{j}=1)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)\cdot\mathbb{P}(O_{n+1}=1)\leq\prod_{j=1}^{n+1}\mathbb{P}(O_{j}=1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.8" class="ltx_p">It then suffices to show that</p>
<table id="A2.EGx75" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(O_{n}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)\leq\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1|O_{n+1}=0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(O_{n}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1)\leq\mathbb{P}(O_{1}=1,\dots,O_{n}=1|O_{n+1}=0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.9" class="ltx_p">which is trivial because the event <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j}(j\in[n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{i}=y_{j}(j\in[n])</annotation></semantics></math> becomes more likely conditioned of the event that <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{i}\neq y_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{i}\neq y_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the inequality holds for <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{J}|=n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{J}|=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and we complete the verification of <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> being negatively correlated. Therefore, using the Chernoff bound for negatively-correlated random variables, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx76" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})\geq 1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{\{M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]-k\}^{2}}{2M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(D\geq k|S=s_{0})\geq 1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{\{M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]-k\}^{2}}{2M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.10" class="ltx_p">Substituting (<a href="#A2.E3" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ B.3 Proof of Theorem (Lower Bound of BoN) ‣ Appendix B Proof of Lower Bounds ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>) and (<a href="#A2.E4" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ B.3 Proof of Theorem (Lower Bound of BoN) ‣ Appendix B Proof of Lower Bounds ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>) into (<a href="#A2.E2" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ B.3 Proof of Theorem (Lower Bound of BoN) ‣ Appendix B Proof of Lower Bounds ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx77" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\mathbb{P}(D\geq k)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\mathbb{P}(D\geq k)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}}\cdot\bigg[1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{\{M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]-k\}^{2}}{2M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]}\bigg)\bigg]\cdot\bigg[1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{(Np-s_{0})^{2}}{2Np}\bigg)\bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{2\sqrt{p}}\cdot\bigg[1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{\{M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]-k\}^{2}}{2M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]}\bigg)\bigg]\cdot\bigg[1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{(Np-s_{0})^{2}}{2Np}\bigg)\bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.11" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2k,s_{0}=3k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2k,s_{0}=3k</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/k}/4\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/k}/4\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4k/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4k/N</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx78" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}=1-\Big(1-\frac{1}{2k}\Big)^{3k}\geq 1-e^{-1.5}\geq\frac{3}{4}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}=1-\Big(1-\frac{1}{2k}\Big)^{3k}\geq 1-e^{-1.5}\geq\frac{3}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.12" class="ltx_p">We thus have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx79" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{\{M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]-k\}^{2}}{2M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{\{M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]-k\}^{2}}{2M[1-(1-1/M)^{s_{0}}]}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq 1-\exp\Big(-\frac{(2k\cdot 3/4-k)^{2}}{2\cdot 2k\cdot 3/4}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq 1-\exp\Big(-\frac{(2k\cdot 3/4-k)^{2}}{2\cdot 2k\cdot 3/4}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=1-e^{-k/12}\geq 1-e^{-1/12}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-e^{-k/12}\geq 1-e^{-1/12},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.13" class="ltx_p">where the second inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We also have <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Np=4k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Np=4k</annotation></semantics></math>, so</p>
<table id="A2.EGx80" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle 1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{(Np-s_{0})^{2}}{2Np}\bigg)=1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{(4k-3k)^{2}}{2\cdot 4k}\bigg)=1-e^{-k/8}\geq 1-e^{8}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{(Np-s_{0})^{2}}{2Np}\bigg)=1-\exp\bigg(-\frac{(4k-3k)^{2}}{2\cdot 4k}\bigg)=1-e^{-k/8}\geq 1-e^{8},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.14" class="ltx_p">where the last inequality holds because <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining all the above, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx81" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{\sqrt{4k/N}}\cdot(1-e^{-1/12})\cdot(1-e^{-1/8})\geq 0.004\sqrt{\frac{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{k}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.004</mn><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}\geq\frac{\epsilon_{\text{RM}}}{\sqrt{4k/N}}\cdot(1-e^{-1/12})\cdot(1-e^{-1/8})\geq 0.004\sqrt{\frac{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{k}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.15" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, the regret is lower bounded by <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx82" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Regret}\geq\Omega\Big(\min\Big\{1,{\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/{k}}}\Big\}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Regret</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Regret}\geq\Omega\Big(\min\Big\{1,{\sqrt{N\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/{k}}}\Big\}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.16" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> (General Lower bound</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first provide a more general version of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(x)\geq\max\{k,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(x)\geq\max\{k,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any positive integer <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in[k,C^{*}(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in[k,C^{*}(x)]</annotation></semantics></math> and any algorithm <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> that outputs <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses, there exists a hard instance <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi^{*},r^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the coverage is <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimation error is <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the regret of algorithm <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.EGx83" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)\geq\frac{M-k}{M}\sqrt{\frac{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{M-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Regret}(x)\geq\frac{M-k}{M}\sqrt{\frac{C^{*}(x)\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{M-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\geq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\geq 2k</annotation></semantics></math>, we can set <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=2k</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain the regret lower bound of <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/k})</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>. We now present the proof of Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem B.2. ‣ B.4 Proof of Theorem (General Lower bound ‣ Appendix B Proof of Lower Bounds ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem B.2. ‣ B.4 Proof of Theorem (General Lower bound ‣ Appendix B Proof of Lower Bounds ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider the case of <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and omit the prompt <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}(x,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}(x,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 General Lower Bound ‣ Best-of-Majority: Minimax-Optimal Strategy for Pass@𝑘 Inference Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, we apply the idea of averaging hammer, and consider a total of <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> hard instances such that no algorithm can perform well on all instances. All of these hard instances have a total of <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M+1</annotation></semantics></math> possible responses <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{y_{0},\dots,y_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{y_{0},\dots,y_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we aim to make <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\dots,y_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\dots,y_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> hard to distinguish from each other. In detail, all hard instances also share the same reference model and the same <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx84" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-M/C,\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{1})=\cdots=\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{M})=1/C;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{0})=1-M/C,\quad\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{1})=\cdots=\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{M})=1/C;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{1})=\cdots=\widehat{r}(y_{M})=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(y_{0})=0,\quad\widehat{r}(y_{1})=\cdots=\widehat{r}(y_{M})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">For hard instance <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{j}(j\in[M])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{j}(j\in[M])</annotation></semantics></math>, we make <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> the optimal response with ground truth reward being <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(y_{j})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(y_{j})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and make all other responses suboptimal with a gap of <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{j}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi_{j}^{*},r_{j}^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{j}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},\pi_{j}^{*},r_{j}^{*},\pi_{\text{ref}},\widehat{r})</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx85" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{j}^{*}(y_{l})=\delta_{jl},\quad r_{j}^{*}(y_{l})=\begin{cases}0&amp;l=0;\\
1&amp;l=j;\\
1-\delta&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{j}^{*}(y_{l})=\delta_{jl},\quad r_{j}^{*}(y_{l})=\begin{cases}0&amp;l=0;\\
1&amp;l=j;\\
1-\delta&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">In this hard instance, the coverage is <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and in order to make the estimation error equal to <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we require</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx86" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(M-1)\cdot\delta^{2}\cdot 1/C=\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(M-1)\cdot\delta^{2}\cdot 1/C=\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">which indicates that <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\sqrt{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/(M-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\sqrt{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}/(M-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since any algorithm can only output a maximum of <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> different responses, it cannot output the optimal response in at least <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M-k</annotation></semantics></math> out of the <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> hard instances, suffering from the regret of at least <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the averaged regret of the <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> instances is at least</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx87" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\sim A}\big[r_{j}^{*}(y_{j})-\max\big\{r_{j}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{1}),\cdots,r_{j}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{k})\big\}\big]\geq\frac{1}{M}\cdot(M-k)\cdot\delta=\frac{M-k}{M}\sqrt{\frac{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{M-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\sim A}\big[r_{j}^{*}(y_{j})-\max\big\{r_{j}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{1}),\cdots,r_{j}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{k})\big\}\big]\geq\frac{1}{M}\cdot(M-k)\cdot\delta=\frac{M-k}{M}\sqrt{\frac{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{M-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, there exists an instance <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{j^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{j^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the <math id="A2.SS4.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> hard instances such that</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx88" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\sim A}\big[r_{j^{*}}^{*}(y_{j^{*}})-\max\big\{r_{j^{*}}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{1}),\cdots,r_{j^{*}}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{k})\big\}\big]\geq\frac{M-k}{M}\sqrt{\frac{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{M-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{y}_{1},\dots,\widetilde{y}_{k}\sim A}\big[r_{j^{*}}^{*}(y_{j^{*}})-\max\big\{r_{j^{*}}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{1}),\cdots,r_{j^{*}}^{*}(\widetilde{y}_{k})\big\}\big]\geq\frac{M-k}{M}\sqrt{\frac{C\epsilon_{\text{RM}}^{2}}{M-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
